# Canonical multi-platform build runner

Implement `tools/build/adapter-inbound/run.py` to consume saved preflight and
architecture
decisions, revalidate them, build every selected target transactionally, and
publish only the minimal native deliverable under `dist/<ARCH>/` for each
successful architecture.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Produce Android ARM64 as APK, iOS ARM64 as IPA, and native packaged
  outputs for selected Linux ARM64/AMD64, macOS ARM64, and Windows
  ARM64/AMD64 architectures.
- [x] Keep tvOS and macOS Intel / x86-64 outside the planned build matrix unless
  a future high-traction issue or pull request brings enough testing support to
  admit them explicitly.
- [x] Keep iOS packaging local and sideload-oriented with no App Store
  submission workflow; document unavoidable Apple SDK, signing, or
  build-host constraints instead of hiding them.
- [x] Use candidate output followed by atomic publication so a failed target
  never appears as a complete `dist/<ARCH>/` result.
- [x] Keep cook, staging, logs, manifests, and tool state under `.cache/`; keep
  final `dist/` output minimal and platform-native.
- [x] Make each published `dist/<ARCH>/` self-contained enough to copy elsewhere
  before deleting the repository workspace if the user wants.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency is required beyond the declared repository and
platform prerequisites.

## Evidence

`tools/build/adapter-inbound/run.py` now validates exact
`shar.build.check.v1` and
`shar.build.arch.v1` decisions, reruns `check.py --revalidate`, and rejects
duplicate
or drifted target records, and verifies each selected platform through Turnkey
without `UpdateIfNeeded` before invoking `BuildCookRun`. The canonical matrix
maps x86-64 to Unreal `x64`, ARM64 to `arm64`, and Linux ARM64 to the dedicated
`LinuxArm64` target; tvOS and macOS Intel are not admitted.

Direct runner invocation now delegates the saved architecture decision back to
`arch.py --revalidate` before consuming any target records. Explicit
architecture selection also rejects repeated target identifiers instead of
silently
deduplicating them before persistence. Focused regressions prove the exact saved
path is revalidated, a failed architecture revalidation stops the runner, and
`main()` performs that gate before target consumption. Architecture and
preflight
evidence are consumed from byte snapshots proven unchanged across their child
revalidation process, so post-validation file replacement cannot substitute a
different saved decision before the runner parses it.

Mobile publication now consumes the declared artifact kind instead of treating
any archive file as success. Android APKs must be valid ZIP containers with an
ARM64 ELF native library, while iOS IPAs must declare a main application
executable in `Info.plist` whose Mach-O image contains ARM64. Invalid
containers,
wrong-architecture mobile binaries, and zero-byte packages cannot create a
completed `dist/<ARCH>/` target. Universal Mach-O validation consumes every
declared architecture record before accepting ARM64, and each declared slice
must have nonzero in-bounds file data after the architecture table. Selected
ARM64 slices must themselves begin with an ARM64 thin Mach-O header. Truncated,
empty, or mislabeled fat slices cannot masquerade as complete macOS or iOS
runtimes. Windows candidates
likewise require a non-empty
SHAR `.exe`; engine helper executables alone cannot satisfy publication. The
optional Windows shortcut adapter applies the same SHAR executable identity, so
original-game, helper, empty, prefixed-helper, or non-Shipping `.exe` files are
never auto-selected. Shortcut auto-discovery also requires `dist/` itself to
remain a real directory, so a later symlink or junction cannot redirect the
helper outside the published tree.
Cached UAT manifests and Windows debug symbols are now required to be regular
files before they can leave the candidate tree; directory entries that merely
match `Manifest_*.txt` or `*.pdb` fail closed instead of becoming cached build
evidence. Every matching entry is type-checked before stale diagnostic caches
are pruned or any new diagnostic is moved, so malformed candidate evidence
preserves the previous successful cache. The required native/mobile artifact is
validated before those caches are replaced as well, so an otherwise well-formed
but non-runnable package cannot displace diagnostics from the last accepted
build.
Artifact entries also require one filesystem link at this boundary instead of
relying only on the earlier tree scan.
Linux candidates now require a non-empty SHAR native executable for both x64
and ARM64, and macOS candidates require a `.app` bundle with a non-empty SHAR
executable below `Contents/MacOS`. On POSIX hosts those native runtimes must
also retain execute permission. Desktop entrypoints must identify their native
binary format as ELF, Mach-O, or PE as appropriate, and the native header must
match the selected x64 or ARM64 target. A renamed executable or a binary for the
wrong architecture cannot satisfy publication. Plain data-only archives can no
longer be
published as successful desktop packages on those platforms.

Publication also preflights an existing `dist/<ARCH>/` identity as a real
directory before any rename. A regression proves a colliding regular file is
left unchanged and cannot trigger a failed cleanup after a new package appears.
The hidden `.previous` publication identity is now checked for existence even
when its link target is missing,
so a broken symlink or other malformed stale backup cannot be ignored and left
beside an otherwise successful target. Rejection happens before the candidate
moves.

The `dist/` publication root is also required to be a real directory rather
than a symbolic link or Windows junction. A regression proves publication
rejects a linked output root before moving the candidate package.

The candidate side of the transaction is likewise required to remain a real
cache-owned directory. A regression proves a linked candidate is rejected
before it can become a `dist/<ARCH>/` symlink to external content.

Project-state migration now validates both `.cache` and `.cache/build` as real
repository directories before creating or adopting Unreal-generated state.
Regressions cover linked cache ancestors so build state cannot escape the repo.

Canonical dependency, preflight, and architecture evidence writers now reject
linked `.cache`, `.cache/build`, or `.cache/build/data` ancestors before any
mutation. Rejection preserves existing evidence; architecture and preflight test
output overrides remain independent. Dependency bootstrap additionally requires
real `.dependencies` and `.dependencies/build` tool storage even when its JSON
evidence path is overridden, because Cargo, rustup, and validator outputs remain
repository-owned side effects. Published validator identities must also remain
real single-link files. Preflight repeats the real-directory and single-link
checks before trusting either validator, so post-bootstrap redirects or
hard-link
aliases cannot move binary authority outside repository-owned storage.
Canonical `arch.json`, `check.json`, and `dependencies.json` identities are also
required to be real regular files when already present. Build preflight repeats
that rule when consuming `dependencies.json`, so a post-bootstrap symlink or
junction cannot redirect dependency authority to external JSON. Preflight parses
and hashes one stable byte snapshot and requires the canonical dependency file to
retain those bytes through source and engine validation, preventing a
mid-check
replacement from binding saved evidence to JSON that did not select the
validators. Linked or malformed saved evidence now fails before it can be
consumed, replaced, or cleaned up.
Their PID-named atomic staging files are now created exclusively. A pre-existing
staging identity is preserved and causes the writer to fail closed instead of
being truncated or removed before the final atomic replace.
The shared `.cache/build/run` directory and each per-target work root are also
required to be real directories before Turnkey or UAT writes reports, logs,
staging data, or candidate packages.
Candidate and staging scratch roots are now preflighted before cleanup and
recreation too. Linked or malformed stale scratch identities fail before UAT,
rather than surviving ignored cleanup and reaching packaging.
Each UAT invocation now also validates its own child write roots: existing log
files must be real files with exactly one filesystem link, while
`automation-saved/`, its `logs/` child, and the local DDC must be real
directories. Linked identities fail before UAT starts, so cached tool output
cannot be redirected through a pre-existing work-tree identity. Turnkey SDK
evidence is likewise required to be a real report file with one filesystem link
both before stale-report cleanup and after UAT returns. Redirected or
hard-linked
`turnkey.txt` evidence cannot make external content satisfy the SDK-validity
gate. The report must decode as UTF-8 and contain a platform-anchored valid SDK
row; a prefixed lookalike platform name cannot satisfy another target's gate.
After UAT returns, the complete candidate tree is traversed without following
links and rejects symbolic links, junctions, hard-linked regular files, or
special filesystem entries before manifests/debug symbols are moved. The final
publication transaction repeats that tree gate, so a direct or drifted caller
cannot publish a redirected candidate between cache pruning and the atomic swap.

A real Windows AMD64 Shipping integration build completed successfully through
C++ compilation, cook, Pak/IoStore, stage, package, and archive in 29m10s. Its
unpublished test candidate contained 30 files totaling about 689 MiB, including
`shar.exe`, project content, required Engine files, manifests, and notices. The
integration driver intentionally stopped before `_publish`, so it did not create
`dist/` or persist a test architecture choice.

Fixture validation proved empty candidates cannot replace an existing published
target and valid candidates replace it transactionally with rollback support.
An injected failure while deleting the post-swap previous-package backup now
rolls the swap back as well: the prior `dist/<ARCH>/` tree is restored, the new
package returns to its cache candidate path, and no failed run leaves the new
publication exposed.
Turnkey validation also confirmed the installed Windows x64 SDK as valid. Build
preflight now requires the host-native Unreal editor identity to be a regular
file rather than merely an existing filesystem entry and, on POSIX hosts, to
have execute permission. The runner similarly requires its host `RunUAT`
launcher to be a non-linked file and executable on POSIX before Turnkey starts.
Directory, redirected, or non-executable launchers cannot become build
authority. UAT logs are redirected beneath
`.cache/build/run/<target>/automation-saved/`. A
second real Shipping build completed successfully in 16m58s with the local DDC
and Zen data rooted under `.cache`; its stage and archive matched the first
successful package size. The packaged Shipping executable remained alive for an
8-second NullRHI/no-audio/unattended liveness probe, after which the exact test
PID was terminated cleanly.

Project-generated `Binaries/`, `DerivedDataCache/`, `Intermediate/`, and
`Saved/` now resolve through persistent directory links to
`.cache/build/project-state/`. Migration preflights all four identities, adopts
legacy real directories by same-filesystem rename, rejects competing canonical
state, and rolls back earlier moves if a later link fails. Standard-library
regressions cover adoption, reuse, dual-authority rejection, and injected
partial failure. On the real Windows workspace the first `Intermediate` rename
hit a transient `WinError 5`; rollback restored all four original directories,
and one controlled retry migrated all four successfully. A pinned Python 3.14.6
`run.py --validate-only` then revalidated build evidence and Turnkey reported
`windows-x64` SDK valid with all four junctions still targeting the cache.

The dependency bootstrap now also binds `validate-game` evidence to a SHA-256
fingerprint of its local Rust source closure, workspace manifest, and lockfile.
Source-closure traversal is explicit and strict in both bootstrap and preflight,
so an unreadable nested source directory cannot silently disappear from the
fingerprint under recursive-glob error suppression. Redirected or special source
entries also fail instead of being omitted from the digest, preventing a source
file reached through a symlink or junction from changing without changing the
recorded closure. Bootstrap fingerprints both validator source closures before
Cargo starts and requires the same fingerprints after both builds and after
publication, so source drift cannot bind a previously built validator to newer
source evidence. This prevents an old byte-intact validator from surviving a
checkout that changes the canonical game-manifest contract; tests prove source
drift, build/publication drift, scan failure, redirect rejection, and legacy
evidence all fail closed.

A current full Windows x64 Shipping run then published `dist/windows-x64`
through the canonical runner. The unpruned publication contained 30 files and
721,654,073 bytes. An exact copy under `.cache/build/copy-probe/` matched every
source path, byte count, and SHA-256; with the original `dist/windows-x64`
temporarily hidden, the copied `shar-Win64-Shipping.exe` remained alive for an
8-second NullRHI/no-audio/unattended probe. The original publication was
restored
in `finally` and the 722 MB probe tree was removed.

The runner now moves UAT `Manifest_*.txt` publication metadata and Windows PDBs
out of the candidate before atomic publication, retaining them under the target
build cache. Those metadata and symbol cache roots are preflighted as real
directories before stale cache cleanup, so redirected identities fail before
any manifest or debug-symbol file is moved. Applying that rule to the accepted
Windows
package reduced final
`dist/windows-x64` to 26 runtime/licensing files and 488,530,675 bytes. A second
exact copy produced inventory SHA-256
`ee8b3f00772c898fab52bf1ae11f81874aea334b840335b94e76fcb7255b4e95`;
the minimal copied Shipping executable again remained alive for eight seconds
while the original `dist` directory was hidden, after which publication was
restored and the copy probe deleted. This verifies the published Windows package
can be copied away from its repository publication path without depending on
that path.

Keep this task open until the remaining platform packages are exercised.
